00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:05.520
MOE SY: Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.190
Thank you for joining the Google
for Games Developer Summit.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:10.340
My name is Mo.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.150
I am a cloud gaming
architect at Google Cloud.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.700
Today I will be joined
by Christina and Jason

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.860
from Unity to talk about
Unity Game Simulation.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.750
If you're a game
developer, you probably

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.690
know Unity, the leading
real-time 3D development

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:29.280
engine that supports over
25 platforms and powers 50%

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.750
of all new games.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.140
What you might not know
is Unity and Google Cloud

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.950
have partnered for years to
make game development easier

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.380
and more accessible
for more creators using

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.510
the power of the cloud.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:48.500
Last fall, Unity announced
a Unity Simulation,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.150
a new cloud-based product that
enables designers, developers,

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.490
and creators to generate
synthetic training

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:59.510
data at scale, as
well as test, train,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.770
and validate all their computer
vision and perception projects

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.940
in Unity by harnessing
the power of the cloud.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.280
Within Google Cloud, Unity
leverages Google's turnkey

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.310
Kubernetes solution, GKE.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.520
GKE, or Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.410
gives Unity developers
a secure way

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.670
to manage and operate
simulations at scale.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.460
GKE provides strong
security defaults,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.180
such as encryption at rest,
encryption in transit,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.230
leading to a more secure
environment for Unity

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.480
developers.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.790
As an added benefit
of running GKE,

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.570
Unity is able to seamlessly
integrate with GCP's ecosystem.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.300
Unity uses Google's
virtual private cloud

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.020
to define its global network,
as well as Google Cloud Identity

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.610
and Access Management to manage
fine-grained access control,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:56.980
each of which have first-class
integrations within GKE.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.590
Today, we're excited to
help our friends at Unity

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.720
announce their latest
innovative product

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.270
in the world of simulation
that's specifically

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.560
tailored for game developers.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.840
I would like to introduce
Christina from Unity

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.180
to talk about Unity
Game Simulation.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.800
CHRISTINA GUO: Thanks, Mo.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.530
Creating games is hard, and
launching a quality game

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.070
is even harder.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.910
Yet the first impression your
game makes on your players

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.850
can determine its
long-term success.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:28.000
To build a quality game, you
need to thoroughly balance it

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.330
prior to launch.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.700
A well-balanced game will
create a fun experience

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.600
for all your players.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.420
You'll appeal to
more players and keep

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.050
them happy and engaged, which
means a higher lifetime value.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.760
So how do you create
a well-balanced game?

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.570
Currently, the answer is
extensive manual balancing.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.030
Through spreadsheets
and human playtesters,

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.570
you'll tediously try to find the
right damage value for a weapon

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.010
or the ideal speed
of a character.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.030
But these are just two of
hundreds of possible parameters

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.750
that you might want to
adjust in your game.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.610
In order to get
these right, you may

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.530
need to spend hours
tweaking values

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.890
in oversimplified and
inaccurate spreadsheets.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.860
Or you contract dozens
of human playtesters,

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.940
but the feedback you receive
is subjective and costly.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.670
Today, we're excited
to announce Unity Game

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.030
Simulation, supported by the
Google Cloud infrastructure.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:27.970
Unity Game Simulation is
an affordable solution

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.560
that complements
your pre-launch play

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.160
tests to provide faster
iteration and results at scale.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.950
The tool allows you to balance
more and iterate on your game

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.700
more often.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.120
Using the online dashboard,
you have the power

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.940
to setup, run, and analyze
thousands of simulations.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.590
Because these
player interactions

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:55.900
run simultaneously, you also
speed up your balancing.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.400
The scale of cloud balancing
provides more accurate results

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.420
than manual balancing
and spreadsheets

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.630
[? can ?] replicate.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.460
This quantitative
balancing coverage

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.490
costs a fraction of a
playtester's hourly rate,

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.620
giving them more time
to focus on the look

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.690
and feel of the game.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:15.470
Let's dive into an example.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.670
Furyion Games, a Canadian
game development studio,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:23.320
created "Death Carnival," a
fast-paced top-down shooter.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.520
One of the features of the game
is the weapon socket system.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.900
Each weapon allows players to
select a base weapon, ammo,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.410
weapon module, and
temporary power-ups.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:37.210
Each of these
combinations is made up

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.610
of many variables that
control the play style

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.550
and efficiency of the web.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.680
In earlier versions
of the game, the team

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.210
balanced weapons by hand.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.080
They would configure a
weapon, compile a build,

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.410
send it out to playtesters,
and watch them play.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.350
Based on the feedback, they
would reconfigure the weapons

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.000
and repeat.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.840
Each iteration of this
process would take hours.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.410
Enter Unity Game Simulation.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:08.080
Furyion used the tool to run
thousands of simulations.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.870
They were able to identify
the right combinations of base

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:14.470
weapons, ammo, weapon
modules, and power-ups

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.760
for their desired survivability
and level completion time.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.690
This allowed them to ensure
the weapons were balanced.

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.870
By configuring the weapon
parameters and multi-stage

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.690
simulations, Furyion
was able to condense

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:33.010
the equivalent of 165
million playthroughs

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:35.550
into just 10
efficient simulations,

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.490
taking just over three days to
balance their weapons system.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:43.420
They estimate that the tool
saved them $80,000 and 600

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.940
hours of dev time.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.740
Those resources
would just previously

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.710
have been spent on managing
playtesters, measuring

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.860
weapon performance,
and compiling builds.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.140
Now, they can instead
be spent on polishing

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:58.330
the creative
elements of the game.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.490
Jason, an engineer
on the team, will now

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.235
demonstrate how Furyion
achieved these results.

00:06:03.235 --> 00:06:05.477
JASON GRECO: After deciding
to use Game Simulation,

00:06:05.477 --> 00:06:07.060
a game developer at
Furyion implements

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:09.490
the Unity Game Simulation
package and uploads

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.993
a build to the game simulation
service from the editor window.

00:06:12.993 --> 00:06:14.410
I won't go through
this in detail,

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.293
but the process is simple.

00:06:16.293 --> 00:06:18.460
Once the developer has
uploaded the "Death Carnival"

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.710
simulation build, it's
time to create a simulation

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:21.420
from the web UI.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.253
This is where designers
configure and run simulations.

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:28.795
For this "Death
Carnival" simulation,

00:06:28.795 --> 00:06:32.230
we will be testing 45 key
combinations of weapons, ammo,

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:33.952
and modules 100
times each in order

00:06:33.952 --> 00:06:36.160
to establish the mean
performance of each combination

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:37.670
with high confidence.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:38.920
Let's name this build Phase 1.

00:06:41.675 --> 00:06:43.550
And we'll select our
build from the dropdown.

00:06:47.465 --> 00:06:48.840
For the values in
our simulation,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:55.330
we'll be testing shotgun,
machine gun, and missile.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:07:02.380
Our ammo count will be
10, 15, 20, 25, and 30.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:04.380
And for our weapon module,
we'll be using triple

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:09.360
shot, wave, and burst.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.810
Once this simulation
is finished,

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.102
we'll get a spreadsheet with
a row for each combination

00:07:13.102 --> 00:07:15.630
of these parameter values.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:20.040
And lastly, we'll run each
combination 100 times.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:27.130
So in total, this will run 4,500
combinations of simulations.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:29.340
So fast forward one
hour, and we can

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.790
see that this simulation
has already completed,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.500
and we have our aggregate data.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.250
So let's open up this data.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:43.612
I've gone ahead and imported
the file into Google Sheets

00:07:43.612 --> 00:07:45.570
and made a chart with
the level completion time

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.782
by weapon combination.

00:07:47.782 --> 00:07:48.990
So here we can see the chart.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.540
And in this chart, we see
the shotgun and machine

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.950
gun both have some pretty
underpowered combinations.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.170
As a next step, I can
buff these combinations

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.357
and run the
simulation once more.

00:08:00.357 --> 00:08:02.190
Furyion has already
done this, so let's take

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.680
a look at the final results.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.260
And we can see that the
underpowered combinations have

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.830
come down pretty drastically in
player completion time, which

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.680
translates to a much better
gameplay experience for "Death

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.720
Carnival's" players.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.523
Now that we've seen Unity
Game Simulation in action,

00:08:18.523 --> 00:08:20.190
let's peel back the
onion a bit and talk

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:23.730
about the underlying
infrastructure.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.160
We chose GKE to build
Unity Game Simulation

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:28.200
on top of because it
gives us the container

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.090
management we wanted.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.660
With Google Compute
Engine, we would

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.169
have needed to manage the
components individually,

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:37.470
such as auto scaling,
networking, security,

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.460
container health, et cetera.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:41.590
With GKE, we just
specify what we want,

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:44.305
and it's managed for us.

00:08:44.305 --> 00:08:46.680
One of the most important
features in building Unity Game

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.710
Simulation is
GKE's auto scaling.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.660
We need the ability to
dynamically scale up

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.670
our number of resources
so we can quickly

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:56.070
handle workloads of
thousands of simulations

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:58.950
without having to pay
for idle resources.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:01.650
Because games all have
different system requirements,

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:04.320
using separate node pools, we're
able to adjust this instance

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.670
type for each game when
we schedule a simulation.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.440
With Unity Game
Simulation, users

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:13.690
tend to run a mix of smaller and
larger batches of simulations,

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:16.980
so it's important for us to
have fast scaling of resources.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.440
The smaller number of
simulations a user is running,

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.870
the more impactful
any extra overhead is,

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:24.360
so being able to quickly
scale up as needed

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.850
lets us handle both
types of workloads.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:31.380
In addition to GKE, we used a
number of other GCP offerings

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.970
in building this tool.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:36.540
One example is Stackdriver,
which we used for our services.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.940
The log aggregation and
metric instrumentation

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:41.712
around GKE and other
Google Cloud resources

00:09:41.712 --> 00:09:43.920
helped minimize the work in
setting up our dashboards

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.590
and alerting as we transition
from our initial proof

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:49.922
of concept into a
production system.

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:52.380
Because we're running user
code, it's also important for us

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.080
to sandbox everything
as much as possible.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.500
With IAM and RBAC, the
user code containers

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.570
can be configured to
run with no permissions

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:05.370
to any GKE or GCP resources,
even on the same node.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.520
IAM also helps secure
our resources and data

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.200
by locking them down to
just a few necessary admins

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.247
for a services so we're
able to create a service

00:10:13.247 --> 00:10:15.330
account for each of them
with the minimum required

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:16.050
permissions.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.758
In addition to GKE and other
products we just mentioned,

00:10:22.758 --> 00:10:24.800
I'd like to quickly cover
some of the other tools

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.290
that we found very useful in
the development of Unity Game

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:28.730
Simulation.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:30.680
First is Cloud SQL,
which we're using

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:32.930
as the database for services
that manage our job state

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:34.940
and orchestration.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.150
Second is GCR, which we're using
along with IAM that I mentioned

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:42.595
earlier for securely managing
all of our Docker containers.

00:10:42.595 --> 00:10:43.970
We also use Dataproc
for handling

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:45.560
the aggregation of
our simulation data

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:48.207
from each of the individual
simulation instances.

00:10:48.207 --> 00:10:49.790
And finally, Terraform,
which helps us

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:54.140
manage all the GCP
infrastructure that we have.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.450
In summary, Unity
Game Simulation

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:59.090
creates a more efficient,
accurate, and affordable

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:00.890
solution for game balancing.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:03.500
It complements playtesting,
backing human feedback

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:06.840
with quantitative data.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:08.420
Try the open beta for free.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:12.650
Visit unity.com/products/game
simulation for more

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:14.660
information, and thank
you all for watching.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:18.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

